WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.980
Deposit insurance stopped the bank run, then paid for a decade of thrift gambling that cost taxpayers $124 billion.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:11.240
This is unintended consequences, episode 96.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:18.720
In March of 1933, Americans queued around the block to yank their savings out of banks that might not open tomorrow.

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:21.760
More than 4,000 banks failed that year.

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.700
Rumor alone could kill the next one down the street.

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:31.480
Half a century later, a money broker in New York could wire millions to a Texas savings in loan he had never seen.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:37.620
The advertised rate was a quarter point higher, and every dollar was guaranteed by the United States.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:44.180
One picture is visceral panic. The other is a rate sheet, a wire transfer, and a government promise.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.980
Deposit insurance was designed to kill the panic that destroyed thousands of banks.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:56.960
Instead, it pot depositors not to ask questions, and it handed phylum thrifts a taxpayer-backed chip with which to gamble.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:00.800
The 1933 picture is why the guarantee was born.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.820
The 1980s picture is what the guarantee quietly taught people to stop doing.

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:15.600
The Banking Act of 1933, signed by Franklin Roosevelt on June 16th, created the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation, the FDIC,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.780
because the alternative had just been lived in public.

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:25.520
Between 1929 and 1933, roughly 9,000 American banks suspended operations.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:33.060
In 1933 alone, more than 4,000 failed. Depositors who got to the window too late lost their money.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.300
The rumor of a rumor was enough to finish the next bank down the street.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:43.080
Representative Henry B. Steagall of Alabama had been pushing a federal guarantee for years.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:48.600
His rural constituents had watched local banks vanish. Roosevelt himself had doubts.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:54.760
He worried that a government promise would invite careless banking and eventually arrayed on the treasury.

00:01:55.420 --> 00:02:00.060
The American Banker's Association fought the idea as a subsidy for weak institutions.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:09.580
They were not being frivolous. Those objections were the arguments of people who had watched banks from the inside, and they were thinking about incentives, not just headlines.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:14.460
But in the spring of 1933, the political fact was simpler than the theory.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:21.420
People would not put money back into banks they could not trust, and the payment system of the United States had already stopped.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:26.320
Steagall's rural constituents and that frozen payment system won the argument.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:37.400
Deposit insurance initially $2,500 per account when the temporary plan took effect on January 1, 1934, was the price of getting deposits to return.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:47.560
A companion fund, the Federal Savings and Loan Insurance Corporation, or FSLIC, followed in 1934 under the National Housing Act.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.240
It wrapped the same logic around the thrifts that financed ordinary home loans.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:03:00.220
The intention was narrow and humane. Stop the run, restore the checking account, let a solvent bank survive a false rumor.

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:04.160
It worked, almost immediately, at the thing it was built to do.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:13.920
In 1933, more than 4,000 banks suspended. In 1934, only 61 failed, and just nine of those were FDIC insured.

00:03:14.660 --> 00:03:21.320
Deposit flowed back. Coverage was made permanent in the banking act of 1935 and raised to $5,000.

00:03:22.020 --> 00:03:30.540
For the next four decades, the American banking system looked from the sidewalk almost boring. Failures were few. Premiums were flat.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:39.500
Every insured bank paid the same rate regardless of how it lent, because the whole point was confidence, not a credit rating agency with a government seal.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:55.480
The ceiling crept up with inflation and politics. $10,000 in 1950. $40,000 in 1974. Then $100,000 in 1980, under the Depository Institution's deregulation and Monetary Control Act.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:01.360
Skeptics had never fully disappeared, including Roosevelt and the American Bankers Association.

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:12.620
Economists and some bankers kept repeating the 1933 objection. If depositors do not care, someone else has to, and that someone is the supervisor, who is always late.

00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:26.020
Those warnings sounded academic while the 363 world of savings and loans still held. Pay 3% on passbook savings, lend at 6% on 30-year mortgages, and be on the golf course by 3.

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:33.620
Regulation boxed thrifts into home mortgages, and interest rates stayed tame enough that the moral hazard stayed mostly latent.

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:46.520
The insurance funds looked fat, the public remembered the bread lines, not the footnotes. Proponents could point to a genuine, measured victory. The contagious bank run as Americans had known it was gone.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:59.320
Then the 1970s broke the box. The guarantee was still there. The watchfulness was not. Inflation and Paul Volcker's Federal Reserve pushed short-term rates toward 20% in 1981.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:10.140
Savings and loans were still stuffed with old fixed-rate mortgages yielding 6% or 8%, while they had to bid for deposits in a market regulation queue could no longer contain.

00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:24.660
On a market-value basis, large parts of the industry were insolvened by the early 1980s. Estimates of the whole ran well into the tens of billions, and some contemporary analyses put the industry's aggregate net worth deeply negative.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:32.360
The straightforward response would have been to close the dead institutions, pay the depositors, and charge the loss to FS-LIC.

00:05:33.040 --> 00:05:54.060
FS-LIC did not have the money. Congress did not want the bill, and the industry had friends. So policy ran the other way. The Garn St. Germain Depository Institutions Act, signed on October 15, 1982, let thrifts load up on commercial real estate, consumer loans, and other assets they had never been staffed to underwrite.

00:05:55.160 --> 00:06:04.860
California's Nolan Act and Texas's liberal state charters went further. Ronald Reagan called Garn St. Germain the most important financial legislation in 50 years.

00:06:05.580 --> 00:06:16.840
It was meant as a life raft. For a thrift that was already broke, it was a lottery ticket bought with insured cash. The victory of 1933 had a mechanism, and the mechanism had a shadow.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:26.280
Once a deposit was insured, the depositors incentive to distinguish a careful thrift from a reckless one collapsed to a single number, the interest rate.

00:06:26.980 --> 00:06:35.800
The bank's incentive shifted to, losses beyond a thin slice of capital belonged to the insurance fund, which is to say, eventually, to the treasury.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:54.660
What followed was not a mystery of markets so much as a predictable use of other people's guarantees. Insolvent zombie thrifts paid above market rates to attract deposits, then through the money at acquisition development construction loans, raw land, and, in the more adventurous shops, junk bonds.

00:06:55.420 --> 00:07:05.320
Deposit brokers in New York and elsewhere sliced large sums into $100,000 certificates of deposit, the insurance ceiling, and shopped the nation for the highest yield.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:11.700
No one had to inspect the loan file in Dallas or Irvine. The government had already inspected the ceiling.

00:07:12.620 --> 00:07:22.040
Vernon Savings in Lone in Texas, run by Don Dixon, became a grim mascot. A French villa, a fleet of aircraft, and a $5,500 tee pot.

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:28.480
When federal conservators finally walked in, they found a loan book that was almost entirely delinquent.

00:07:28.480 --> 00:07:36.480
Lincoln Savings, under Charles Keating, grew from a sleepy orange county thrift into a vehicle for junk bonds and desert real estate.

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:42.820
Lincoln Savings also sold uninsured bonds to thousands of mostly elderly customers in its branches.

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:49.160
Many of those customers believed the bonds were as safe as the insured certificates advertised a few feet away.

00:07:49.820 --> 00:07:57.280
Lincoln's failure cost the government on the order of $3 billion. Across Texas, California, Florida, and the Southwest,

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:09.620
similar books piled up. Accounting forbearance and regulatory accounting principles let institutions count supervisory goodwill and other fictions as capital, so the zombies could keep growing.

00:08:10.400 --> 00:08:17.180
Edward Kane and other economists described the result as a put option. Heads, the owners, and managers won.

00:08:17.180 --> 00:08:29.020
Tales, FS-LIC paid. By 1987, the FS-LIC fund itself was insolvent. In 1988 and 1989, the failures came in waves.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:35.680
From the mid-1980s through the mid-1990s, more than 1,000 thrifts were closed or resolved.

00:08:36.160 --> 00:08:46.060
The Resolution Trust Corporation, the RTC, created in 1989, would eventually take over hundreds of them and dispose of hundreds of billions of dollars in assets.

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:54.920
Much of it was distressed real estate dumped into a weak market. Second order damage spread through construction employment and local tax bases.

00:08:55.320 --> 00:09:03.080
A political scandal, the Keating-5, showed how the same guarantee that calm depositors could be used to buy time from senators.

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:12.460
Third order, the cleanup itself tightened credit as surviving institutions and the RTC retrenched, adding weight to the early 1990s downturn.

00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:19.560
The FDIC later put the taxpayer cost of the savings and loan crisis at roughly $124 billion.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:26.380
broader tallies that include industry assessments run higher, commonly around $150 billion.

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:40.100
The runs of 1933 had been stopped. The bill arrived with interest in 1989. Congress did not repeal deposit insurance. It tried imperfectly to put the fear back somewhere else.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:54.220
The Financial Institutions Reform Recovery and Enforcement Act of 1989, signed by George H.W. Bush, abolished FSLIC, shifted thrift insurance to the FDIC and created the RTC.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:59.180
It raised capital expectations and restricted some of the wilder asset powers.

00:09:59.820 --> 00:10:04.860
The Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation Improvement Act of 1991 went further.

00:10:04.860 --> 00:10:13.660
Risk-based premiums, so riskier banks would pay more. Prompt corrective action, a ladder of mandatory constraints as capital declined.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:19.540
Leased cost resolution rules and tighter limits on broker deposits for weak institutions.

00:10:20.240 --> 00:10:28.540
Those tools were a confession that the 1933 design had been incomplete. They were also imperfect. Premium still lagged the risk.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:34.440
Capital ratios can be gamed. Prompt corrective action is only as honest as the accounting.

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:42.840
The 2008 crisis revealed that the largest banks carried an implicit guarantee beyond the statutory cap. Too big to fail.

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:48.480
Coverage was raised to $250,000, first as emergency, then as law.

00:10:48.820 --> 00:10:57.280
In March of 2023, when Silicon Valley Bank and Signature Bank collapsed, uninsured depositors were protected after all.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:11:01.660
The theory was that a run on the uninsured would become a run on the system.

00:11:02.320 --> 00:11:09.900
The safety net widened again for reasons that would have sounded familiar to Stegol. Panic is expensive and guarantees are fast.

00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:19.760
The counter measures remain, so does the original bargain. A guarantee that removes fear also removes watchfulness and the watchfulness has to live somewhere.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:25.900
It has to live in premiums that bite, so a reckless book costs more to ensure than a careful one.

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:33.380
It has to live in capital that is real, not supervisory goodwill and other fictions that let an insolvent firm keep growing.

00:11:34.060 --> 00:11:38.740
And it has to live in supervisors who close the dead instead of hoping they earn their way out.

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:45.320
Incentive structures find their loopholes, especially when the loophole is legal and the loss is socialized.

00:11:45.980 --> 00:11:54.620
Forbearance is not mercy. It is a loan of public money at a hidden interest rate, and the borrower who is already insolvent will not use it conservatively.

00:11:54.620 --> 00:11:59.760
Deposit insurance remains one of the great stabilizing inventions of the 20th century.

00:12:00.200 --> 00:12:05.620
The original bargain is still on the table every time a new class of liabilities looks run prone.

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:14.220
In March of 2023, uninsured depositors at Silicon Valley Bank and Signature Bank were protected to stop a wider run.

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:24.280
Coverage had already been lifted to $250,000, and the largest banks already carried an implicit too big to fail promise past the statutory cap.

00:12:24.980 --> 00:12:32.460
Each widening had a reason that would have sounded familiar in 1933. Panic is expensive and guarantees are fast.

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:37.200
That does not make the reason wrong. It makes the residual question sharper.

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:46.440
The next time a regional bank or some new pool of money that people treat like a checking account starts to look run prone, the crowd will want to be calmed.

00:12:47.000 --> 00:12:51.660
What would you watch for before that day arrives? Not whether the guarantee gets written.

00:12:52.300 --> 00:13:03.020
Who exactly is still supposed to stay afraid? The customer who no longer inspects the books, the supervisor who arrives late, or the taxpayer who holds whatever risk is left.

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:12.620
That's the case for today. The lessons tend to travel further when you share them, so send this one to someone who'd find it useful, and subscribe wherever you're listening.

